==============================================================================
CONTROL -- the directed 3-cycle (GLMY: beta_1 = 1)
==============================================================================
  GLMY (regular)     dim Omega = [3, 3, 0, 0, 0]
                     betti     = [1, 1, 0, 0]
  repo (distinct)    dim Omega = [3, 3, 0, 0, 0]
                     betti     = [1, 1, 0, 0]
  -> GLMY beta_1 = 1 reproduces the textbook value, so the implementation
     is calibrated.  Note Omega_3 differs already here: the 3-cycle admits
     (a,b,c,a) as an allowed regular 3-path, excluded by the repo rule.

==============================================================================
THE AUDIT -- Paley T_7, both conventions, degrees 0..4
==============================================================================
  GLMY (regular)     dim Omega_0..5 = [7, 21, 42, 70, 105, 147]
                     betti_0..4     = [1, 0, 0, 0, 0]
  repo (distinct)    dim Omega_0..5 = [7, 21, 42, 63, 63, 42]
                     betti_0..4     = [1, 0, 0, 0, 6]

==============================================================================
VERDICT
==============================================================================
  Omega_0, Omega_1, Omega_2 agree : True
  first degree where dim Omega differs : 3  (GLMY 70 vs repo 63)
  beta_2 under GLMY : 0   (repo: 0)
  beta_4 under GLMY : 0   (repo canon reports 6)

  If Omega_{0,1,2} agree then beta_1 <= 1 (THM-103) and beta_2 = 0 (THM-108)
  are CONVENTION-SAFE and survive.  If beta_4 differs from 6, the degree->=3
  results -- THM-129 chi(T_p) = p, THM-130's Paley Betti formula, THM-096's
  and THM-099's degree->=3 tables -- are computed in the wrong complex.
